[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608300100-a-failure-keeps-its-name-its-status-and-its-words"
  :adr/title "A failure keeps its name, its status and its words"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-30"
  :adr/body
  "## What was measured

2026-08-30, on the resident deployment. `bots workforce` answered:

    bots 126 / enabled 126 / businesses 21
    outcomes, last 50 resident runs (2026-08-29T10:43Z -> 2026-08-30T03:04Z):
      http-error 40 / fallback-failed 8 / running 2   -- zero successes

Sixteen hours, fifty runs, nothing completed. The question an operator has to
answer next is whether to look at the model provider or at this application.
Nothing in the store could answer it, for three separate reasons, each of
which is a name for the same defect: a failure lost information on the way
from the wire to the person reading about it.

## The three seams

**1. The words were never read.** The resident Goal path streams --
`send-stream!` -> `agent-turn-stream!` -> `streaming-response` -- and that arm
threw `:provider/http-error` carrying `:status` and `:url` and nothing else.
It never opened the response body. `request-json`, the non-streaming arm,
supplies `:response`, and `error-message` two layers up is written to render
exactly that -- it parses OpenRouter's doubly-nested `:metadata :raw`, falls
back to a cause's ex-data, and bounds the result to 120 characters. All of
that machinery was reachable and was handed nothing on the path the workforce
actually takes. Every one of the 40 failures was stored as the same nine
words.

**2. The words that did exist were deleted.** `:agent.run/error-message` was
added to the failure path on 2026-08-21, after a measurement found it written
zero times against 141 for `:turn/error-message` -- \"a type alone cannot
distinguish a misconfigured provider from an overloaded one.\" It was never
added to `compact-run-keys` in `gc.cljc`, whose docstring enumerates the
readers of a terminal run. Compaction dropped it two days later, and has
dropped it since. Measured on the store: 1,079 runs carry an error type; 108
still carry its message. The 2026-08-21 fix has been live and ineffective for
nine days.

**3. The name lost its namespace at the operator's surface.**
`resident-outcomes` counts under the FULL name on purpose, with a comment
explaining that `json/write-str` renders a keyword as its name alone. The
server sends `\"provider/http-error\"` as a string, correctly. `app-client`
parses the response with `:key-fn keyword`, and `cli/-main` wrote it back out
with `json/write-str` -- turning the string back into a keyword and then
printing its name. `bots workforce` therefore reported `{\"http-error\": 40}`,
which is indistinguishable from another namespace's `http-error` and from a
bare one. The server's care was undone one layer out, in the surface an
operator and an agent actually read.

## The decision

A failure reaches the operator with its name, its status and its words.

- `streaming-response` reads a bounded excerpt of a refused body and throws it
  as `:response`, in the same shape `request-json` uses: parsed JSON when it
  parses, `{:raw text}` when it does not, so one extraction downstream answers
  both and a Cloudflare error page survives as text rather than as nothing.
  The bound is 8 KiB and is named: a refusal is an error document, not a
  generation, and an unbounded read of a stream a broken origin controls does
  not belong inside a `throw`.
- `compact-run-keys` keeps `:agent.run/error-message`. It is bounded to one
  120-character line by `error-message` itself, which is what makes it
  affordable to keep for the life of the run.
- `cli/-main` walks its result and gives every keyword the name it actually
  has, before writing JSON. A walk rather than `:key-fn`/`:value-fn`: those
  two reach map keys and map values, and a keyword inside a vector would still
  be stripped. A guarantee with a hole in it reads exactly like a whole one.

## What this does not do

It does not fix the outage. It makes the outage legible. Reproduced against
`api.murakumo.cloud` the same hour, non-streaming and streaming, with and
without a 12 KB 28-tool payload: HTTP 200 every time, 0.9 s to 5.7 s, real
generation. Whatever the 40 refused requests differ in, the answer is in a
response body that has never been recorded. The next reading of
`bots workforce` will say which subsystem refused and in what words; this one
could not.

## Related

- ADR-2608280300, the layer below kept it and the layer above dropped it.
- ADR-2608280200, the failure path is not the path you fixed. This is the
  third instance in the series, and the first where all three seams were on
  one request at once.
"}]
